    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d199">

                <pb facs="0373" n="287" sameAs="frus:frus1945Malta/pg287"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">Roosevelt Papers: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Prime Minister Churchill to President Roosevelt
                    </hi><anchor n="1" xml:id="d199-f1"/>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#center">[Excerpts]<anchor n="2" xml:id="d199-f2"/>
                    </seg>
                </opener>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">top secret</hi>
                    </seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-11-27">27 November
                        1944</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Prime Minister to President Roosevelt
                    Personal and Top Secret Number 834.</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>3. Your last paragraph.<anchor n="3" xml:id="d199-f3"/> We have not got to this
                    point yet and I agree with you we should collect a good many arms from the
                    Germans. <pb facs="0374" n="288" xml:id="pg288"/> Still, I think when American
                    divisions begin to return home there would be a strong case for their leaving
                    some of their heavy weapons and equipment behind for the French to take over the
                    job.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Prime</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d199-f1" n="1" type="source" xml:id="d199-f1-content">
                <p>Sent by the United States Military Attaché, London, via Army channels.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d199-f2" n="2" xml:id="d199-f2-content">
                <p>Other portions of this telegram are printed <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>,
                        <ref target="#pg19">p. 19</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d199-f3" n="3" xml:id="d199-f3-content">
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Supra</hi>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
